How to read 1/1
---------------

A batch of one is not a rate. Four 100%s over a single requirement say that
nothing went wrong once; they say nothing about how often something does. The
rates worth citing are the twenty-item ones in
docs/pipeline-runs/2026-08-13-split-pipeline.md. What this run measures is that
the stages compose, not how well they perform.

What a second run of the same requirement produced
--------------------------------------------------

A different contract. Same requirement, same prompts, S1–S4 all valid again,
and the interface came out `ParkingLot::GateControl` with operations
`get_floor_availability` / `get_barrier_state` / `open_entry_barrier`, against
this run's `ParkingFacility::ParkingControl` with `get_floor_occupancy` /
`get_gate_status` / `open_entry_gate`. The scope moved too: this run wrote the
`gate:operate` the requirement names, the second wrote
`parkinglot.barrier.open`. See the run record for what that costs and why it is
the finding the demonstration exists to have produced.
